Pipe shells' mitred corners: the sides extended past the corner as the
prisms of their end sections (mitcad#1559).

For a mitred corner (BRepFill_Right) BRepFill_Sweep::BuildShell extends
the surface of each side past the corner by EvalExtrapol's length, by
GeomLib::ExtendSurfByLength at C1 continuity: from the approximated
surface's derivatives at its end. Along a curved piece whose frame turns
about it those derivatives lean, and the extensions of the two sides of a
3 degree corner between two B-spline pieces, 0.22 mm long, passed each
other by up to 0.026 mm where they cross the bisector plane: the mitre cut
there (1663) could not join them. A B-spline side is now extended
straight along the normal of its section at the law's end: rows of poles
of the end row translated along it (with the end row's weights), so the
extension is the prism of the end section, as a mitre's sides are. The
extension joins the surface at C0 (a knot of the surface's degree in
multiplicity). Other surfaces (the analytic ones of lines and circles)
are extended as before.

With it the cuts of that corner's sides lie 2.7e-5 mm apart and the pipe
is mitred square to its path (it leaned by 3 degrees). The corners of the
existing mitred pipes and sweeps keep their volumes.

